{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on request 957c6814, identifiers (c+d): the two rollout\ncontracts of section 8. The implementation reads no list of rooms. A\nstore says which format it holds, and the code reads only its own.\n\n1. The format word of a room. The store of a scope object holds one\n   word for its record format. The new format writes the value 2 when\n   it first sets up an empty database, in the same setup. No older\n   store holds the word.\n   a. The object reads the word before it constructs `SqliteStore` and\n      before any schema helper runs. An empty database is fresh. A\n      database that holds any table and no word 2 is a retired room.\n   b. A retired room constructs no store, runs no helper and writes\n      nothing. Its bytes stay as the last old code left them. There is\n      no translation, no added column and no rebuild.\n   c. The check costs its reads once for each start of an object, not\n      once for each request. State the count in the note.\n   d. An object that old code touched and never gave an entry is\n      retired too. A new seed gives a new scope ID, so I expect no new\n      creation to meet one. If a fixed or derived scope ID can, name\n      it.\n\n2. The refusal. Every entry of a retired room answers before any\n   other work.\n   a. HTTP: status 410, the existing error body, the error word\n      `room-retired`, and one member `reader` with the full commit of\n      item 3. The message says that the room retired at the identifier\n      cutover and that this commit reads its journal.\n   b. A call from another object: one closed stop, `retired`. Each\n      caller maps it to an answer that it has today, never to data and\n      never to a recorded judgment. List the mapping of each method in\n      the note.\n   c. The alarm: it does no work and sets no alarm. No held send\n      leaves a retired room.\n   d. `room-retired` joins `contract` as a refusal word. The report\n      says so.\n\n3. The reader commit. One exported constant in source holds the full\n   hash of the last old-format main commit. Its value is the main\n   commit that the cutover head integrates before its gate. I hold\n   main from that integration to the landing, so the first parent of\n   the landing is that commit. The checker's landing check compares\n   the two. If main moves, the head integrates again and the constant\n   changes with it.\n\n4. The inventory. It does not exist yet, and it stays my obligation\n   before the deployment: one act that lists each room's Worker, scope\n   ID, last entry and archive state, and the commit of item 3. The\n   code consumes nothing from that act.\n\n5. The command line. The fresh format is `v: 2` in config.json. The\n   reader in packages/cli/src/files.ts accepts 2 and nothing else,\n   with a check of the shape and no cast.\n   a. A home with `v: 1` is refused before any signing and before any\n      use of a target. The message says that the home names rooms of\n      the old format, that they retired, which commit reads them, and\n      that a new `DAP_HOME` joins a room of the new format.\n   b. The command changes nothing in that home: the config, the keys\n      and every custody record under it stay as they are. Nothing is\n      translated, deleted or sent again.\n   c. `dap mcp` reads the same config and gives the same refusal.\n\n6. The other stores. Before you build for them, send me one list of\n   every other store that holds an old reference: hosted custody, the\n   Page's `Kept`, `AttemptJournal` and session records, the Site token\n   rows and the scope index. For each, say whether a row of a retired\n   room can be read for a new room. A store where it cannot needs\n   nothing. I rule on the others from the list.\n\nDocs in the head: one short section in docs/operate-a-service.md that\ntells an operator how to read a retired room at the reader commit.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}